  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This guide is for the updated version of the Shadar-Kai published in Monsters of the Multiverse. For previous versions of the Shadar-Kai, see our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Elf Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/elf/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The philosophical opposite of eladrin, shadar-kai are elves who live in the Shadowfell. As suits their home plane, shadar-kai are typically somber."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Shadar-Kai published in Monsters of the Multiverse is very similar to the version in Mordenkainen’s Tome of Foes. Fey Ancestry and Trance were both updated to the new versions,  but their theme and defining traits weren’t changed conceptually."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Compared to the Eladrin, the Shadar-Kai trades the ability to change your teleportation’s rider effect for durability. This makes the Shadar-Kai more durable, though certainly less flashy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gift of the Raven Queen is notably not a spell, which means that it can’t be counterspelled, works for characters who have trouble casting spells like barbarians, and won’t prevent you from casting a leveled spell as an action."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
